## Stop and pivot rules

- both depth-two completions: proceed to the autonomous-reuse stage under the
  same cause architecture; run selector/source only if both survive that
  stage;
- exactly one completion: record a scoped depth-two consistency selector
  between these two supplied couplings, without claiming global or autonomous
  uniqueness, and then test its reuse;
- the correlated table needs four shared states but the declared finite
  Record carrier cannot supply or debit them: pivot to causal Record history;
- an abstract cause exists but one named `M_2(C)` encoding, routing, or factor
  map fails: dispatch only that map to the compiler;
- depth two succeeds but exact all-use reuse has a proved growing
  memory/entropy requirement: pivot to extensive history;
- both fail only on cadence, invocation, or formation rate: make no coupling
  selection and split out an occurrence-law theorem; and
- timeout or incomplete classification: no scientific inference.

Only after a normalized directed intensity, conserved source/debit current,
and a lawful source decoder exist should PR `#7803` be joined.  If the pair
fork itself is to be visible to gravity, that later bridge must carry a pair
correlator rather than only additive one-tip data.

